  "title": "Fear Conditioning: Learning to Be Afraid",
  "emoji": "❤️",
  "color": "#9333EA",
  "description": "A 60-second lesson on Fear Conditioning: Learning to Be Afraid.",
  "is_free": false,
  "published": true,
  "story": {
    "hook": {
      "text": "A tone plays. Then a shock comes. After a few pairings, the tone alone makes you freeze. Fear has been learned.",
      "visual": "🔔"
    },
    "buildup": {
      "text": "Pavlovian fear conditioning teaches the amygdala to associate neutral stimuli with threats.",
      "visual": "🧪"
    },
    "discovery": {
      "text": "The learning is incredibly fast—sometimes a single pairing is enough to create a lasting fear memory.",
      "visual": "⚡"
    },
    "twist": {
      "text": "Extinction training can reduce the fear, but it doesn't erase the original memory—it creates a competing one.",
      "visual": "🔇"
    },
    "climax": {
      "text": "Under stress, the original fear memory can resurface—explaining why phobias can return after treatment.",
      "visual": "🔁"
    },
    "punchline": {
      "text": "Fear is easy to learn and hard to forget. That's evolution keeping you cautious.",
      "visual": "🧠"
    }
  },
  "quiz": {
    "question": "Does extinction training erase the original fear memory?",
    "options": [
      "No, it creates a competing memory that suppresses the fear",
      "Yes, it completely removes the memory",
      "It strengthens the original fear"
    ],
    "correct": 0
  },
